==General election==
===Campaign===
This was the second Senatorial race for Gordon Smith in 1996; he had previously lost to [[Ron Wyden]] in the [[1996 United States Senate special election in Oregon|special election]] to fill [[Bob Packwood]]'s seat.

Both candidates spent heavily from their own resources. Bruggere won the [[Democratic Party (United States)|Democratic]] nomination with $800,000 of his own money in the primary race,<ref name=globe>{{cite news | title =  Dignified contest slips a bit; race for Senate takes lower road into negativism | work=The Boston Globe | date=October 1, 1996 | first=Curtis | last=Wilkie}}</ref> and was one of 134 candidates for the U.S. Congress to finance their own elections in excess of $50,000 in that cycle.<ref>{{cite news | title =  More and more candidates financing own campaigns | work=The Seattle Times | date=July 21, 1996 | last=Cass | first=Connie}}</ref> Smith had already spent $2.5 million of his own money earlier that same year in an unsuccessful effort to defeat Democrat [[Ron Wyden]] in the [[United States Senate special election in Oregon, 1996|1996 special election]] to replace [[Bob Packwood]], who had resigned.<ref name=globe/>

Shortly after their respective primary victories, the rivals met for a highly publicized lunch, and agreed to run issue-oriented campaigns. However, in the final weeks of the campaign, Bruggere supporters ran advertisements alleging a pollution problem with Smith's frozen foods business, which the Smith campaign characterized as a breach of that agreement.<ref name=globe/> A ''[[Boston Globe]]'' profile highlighted their similarities as corporate candidates with minimal political experience.<ref name=globe/>

In the general election race, most Oregon daily newspapers endorsed Smith over Bruggere.<ref>{{cite news | title =  Most Oregon dailies pick Smith over Bruggere | work=The Columbian | date=November 3, 1996}}</ref> The race was close, with neither side claiming victory for several days after the election, as [[absentee ballots]] were tallied. After all votes were counted, Smith won by 4 percentage points.<ref>{{cite news | title =  GOP Oregon Senate seat - Tycoon wins close race to succeed Hatfield | work=The Chicago Tribune | date=November 9, 1996 | author=The Associated Press}}</ref> It was the last of the [[United States Senate elections, 1996|1996 Senate elections]] to be determined; overall, the Republicans gained two seats in the Senate, increasing their majority from 53 to 55 seats.<ref>{{cite news | title =  Smith wins tight Oregon Senate race | first=Helen | last=Dewar | newspaper=The Washington Post | date=November 9, 1996}}</ref>

